turnip: darwin, a finished BO's struct cleared under the retire lock before its record can be reused (WindowServer's handle-0 Submit)

- darwin_bo_finish queued a freed BO's retire record under retire->lock (darwin_reap_locked puts it into the cache at once when its fence
  has passed), unlocked, and only then zeroed the tu_bo. A cache hit has the same kernel handle and therefore the same struct
  (tu_device_lookup_bo): another thread's darwin_bo_init in between set the struct up for its own BO, the late memset wiped it, and that
  command buffer submitted handle 0 / iova 0 (ws-diag1: "gpu submit refused: handle unknown: entry 13/128 handle 0 iova 0 bytes 1168"),
  or its later sub-streams carried CPU addresses into the GPU (the slot-1 faults of the PNG -> HEIC wallpaper switch). The trigger is
  the frontend's reclaim (nmb_device_trim_memory(0): command pools destroyed in a burst off the render threads). The struct is now
  cleared under the lock, before the record is queued or reaped: upstream msm's tu_bo_make_zombie order (tu_knl_drm.cc, under vma_mutex).
- A/B on one image: piano-tu-bo-clear=late on the boot line (kern.piano_bootargs, else kern.bootargs), else "late" as the first word of
  /private/var/ngpu/debug/tu-bo-clear, keeps the old order; early is the default. Read once per process, said once at the device's start:
  "NativeAdreno: BO clear early (under the retire lock), from the default" / "... late (after the unlock, the old order), from ...".
- Inert until they fire (the unified log, where a GPU client's line is read without stderr-log=1, and stderr; the first 16 of a kind,
  then the 100th, 1000th, ...):
  "NativeAdreno: BO <h> set up again while still set (...): finish/init race" (darwin_bo_init, release builds have no assert);
  "NativeAdreno: BO <h> handed out again before its finish cleared it (...): the late clear wipes it" (late only);
  "NativeAdreno: cs <name>: current BO cleared (cpu <p>, ...)" (tu_cs_add_entry);
  "NativeAdreno: submit entry <i>/<n> refused: BO cleared under it (offset, bytes; its struct last finished as BO <h> of <size> bytes,
  ...); not sent to the kernel": such an entry is left out of the submission in both modes, instead of a kernel BadArgument and a lost
  device.
Against the tree after 0001-0012. MIT, as the files.

